From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 02:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] shader: EDEN_NX2_PAIRED_POINTERS binds loaded pointers that compete with a paired
 cbuf pointer

Core patch on mig020-core (the v1.219.020 series), off unless EDEN_NX2_PAIRED_POINTERS=1 is set.
Port of Djihads80/Eglantine PR 715, a51a69fbfb (Duck912), merged to nx2-experimental.

A native global access whose address is a select or phi of a constant buffer pointer and a pointer
loaded from that buffer was paired with the constant buffer pointer alone, so the native candidate
search never ran, and accesses through the loaded pointer read out of bounds as zeros. Duck912
reports that Ocarina's near-leaf pass 539ffb3f650a34ac read every placement record as zero and wrote
NaN positions, so trees lost their leaves inside about 20 m.

With the switch the candidate search also runs for paired accesses. If a loaded pointer competes
with the pair, every candidate and the paired pointer are bound and the backend picks at run time;
otherwise the pair stays. Our candidate loop differs from theirs and is not touched. Two differences
from the upstream hunks: the switch, and a limit. PR 715 binds the pair with no check, which at 32
bindings makes 33 and overflows storage_buffers_descriptors. Here a pair that would be the 33rd
stays on the old path, where the MAX_SSBOS check keeps the access global.

Loaded pointers are only found where EDEN_NX2_NATIVE_TABLES allows. Our base env has it at compute,
so in graphics stages the pair stays and the switch does nothing; PR 715 has no such gate. Which
stage the leaf pass is in was not checked. Those leaves are mesh draws, which we skip, so expect no
change before the mesh path runs. It adds bindings to programs near the 32 storage buffer limit, and
run161's log has the limit warning 6 times. The switch changes the SPIR-V of the programs it
touches, so the first launch rebuilds those. Unset, nothing changes. Not built, not run.
---
 .../global_memory_to_storage_buffer_pass.cpp  | 33 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/shader_recompiler/ir_opt/global_memory_to_storage_buffer_pass.cpp b/src/shader_recompiler/ir_opt/global_memory_to_storage_buffer_pass.cpp
index 9d8c26c..56dbe95 100644
--- a/src/shader_recompiler/ir_opt/global_memory_to_storage_buffer_pass.cpp
+++ b/src/shader_recompiler/ir_opt/global_memory_to_storage_buffer_pass.cpp
@@ -1355,7 +1355,22 @@ void CollectStorageBuffers(IR::Block& block, IR::Inst& inst, StorageInfo& info,
         !env.IsCbufStorageBufferPointer(storage_buffer->index, storage_buffer->offset)) {
         storage_buffer.reset();
     }
-    if (native && !storage_buffer && !low_addr.IsImmediate()) {
+    // EDEN_NX2_PAIRED_POINTERS=1 (Djihads80/Eglantine PR 715, Duck912): a paired constant buffer
+    // pointer can be only one input of a select or phi whose other input is a pointer loaded from
+    // that buffer (Ocarina of Time's foliage unit placement records are read from c0[12] or from
+    // *c0[12]). Rebasing on the constant buffer pointer then reads the loaded path's records out of
+    // bounds, as zeros, so look for loaded pointers here as well.
+    static const bool paired_search = [] {
+        const char* const value = std::getenv("EDEN_NX2_PAIRED_POINTERS");
+        return value != nullptr && value[0] != '0';
+    }();
+    // With every binding taken and the pair not bound yet, binding it would make 33 and overflow
+    // storage_buffers_descriptors. PR 715 has no such limit: leave the access to the MAX_SSBOS
+    // check below, as without the switch.
+    const bool paired_found =
+        paired_search && storage_buffer.has_value() &&
+        (info.set.size() < Info::MAX_SSBOS || info.set.find(*storage_buffer) != info.set.end());
+    if (native && (!storage_buffer || paired_found) && !low_addr.IsImmediate()) {
         const IR::Inst* const start{low_addr.InstRecursive()};
         auto it{info.native_candidates.find(start)};
         if (it == info.native_candidates.end()) {
@@ -1368,10 +1383,22 @@ void CollectStorageBuffers(IR::Block& block, IR::Inst& inst, StorageInfo& info,
             }
         }
         const auto& candidates{it->second};
-        if (candidates.size() > 1 || (!candidates.empty() && info.native_dynamic.contains(start))) {
+        const bool has_loaded_pointer{std::ranges::any_of(
+            candidates, [](const StorageBufferAddr& c) { return c.indirect != ~0U; })};
+        if (paired_found && !has_loaded_pointer) {
+            // No pointer loaded from memory competes with the paired one: keep the pair.
+        } else if (candidates.size() > 1 || paired_found ||
+                   (!candidates.empty() && info.native_dynamic.contains(start))) {
             // Bind every candidate and keep the access global; the backend picks the buffer at
             // run time. Rebasing on one of them drops the other path's accesses.
             bool accepted{};
+            if (paired_found) {
+                // The paired pointer is the other input of the select or phi; bind it too.
+                info.set.insert(*storage_buffer);
+                if (IsGlobalMemoryWrite(inst)) {
+                    info.writes.insert(*storage_buffer);
+                }
+            }
             for (const StorageBufferAddr& candidate : candidates) {
                 const StorageBufferAddr holder{.index = candidate.index,
                                                .offset = candidate.offset};
@@ -1404,7 +1431,7 @@ void CollectStorageBuffers(IR::Block& block, IR::Inst& inst, StorageInfo& info,
                 return;
             }
             // Only table entries, and no free binding for them: as if none had been found.
-        } else if (candidates.size() == 1) {
+        } else if (candidates.size() == 1 && !paired_found) {
             // The one pointer the address is built from. The NVN search below only takes 16-byte
             // aligned slots, so for a pointer at an 8-byte slot it can return a dimension or stride
             // the address arithmetic reads instead, and the access then lands in the wrong buffer.
-- 
